- op: discuss  # 9824
  at: '2026-08-27T19:32:54Z'  # 9824
  by: agent  # 9824
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9824
  clock: 3  # 9824
  nonce: '9824'  # 9824
  message: "PREMISE REFUTED IN THE CODE, 2026-08-27 — the root_cause and description\nboth say supply_chain_tier \"is None on EVERY package node in both repos\".\nThat is a NULL-EXTRACTOR ARTIFACT, not a fact about the data.\n\nir.py:585 declares `supply_chain_tier: int = 1` (default first_party), and\nir.py:636-645 serialises it NESTED, under `supply_chain.tier`. A read of\nnode[\"supply_chain_tier\"] at top level therefore returns None for EVERY node\nof EVERY kind — package or not. Same shape as the claims/id vs\nverdicts/claim_id extractor bug.\n\nMEASURED over all 42 cached surveys (lutuh_scope_08242026), 796 package-kind\nnodes across 19 repos:\n    tier 1: 187    tier 3: 609    tier None: 0\nand 10 of the 19 repos carry BOTH tiers, so the field DOES discriminate.\nripgrep example: every one of its 11 package nodes is tier 1 with a real\nreason (\"in workspace globset\", \"in workspace matcher\", ...).\n\nBUT TIER IS STILL NOT THE RIGHT DISCRIMINATOR, for a reason the item did not\nanticipate. Tier on a package node is derived from the DECLARING FILE PATH, so\na genuinely third-party package declared in an in-repo manifest reads\nfirst_party:\n    cmake:CMakeLists.txt:204-204:\"GnuTLS\":package  tier=1  reason=\"default\"\nGnuTLS is not libzmq. Suppressing on tier-1-package would hand a false clean\nverdict to exactly the population this gate protects — the failure direction\nthe item correctly refused to guess at.\n\nSO THE CONCLUSION SURVIVES, WITH A CORRECTED REASON: first-party-ness must come\nfrom the MANIFEST, read directly (Cargo.toml [package] name, go.mod module,\npackage.json name, pyproject [project].name), not from a graph-node property.\nThat is also the cross-language follow-up already documented in\ncli._classify_symbols's own docstring.\n\nNOT REUSABLE, checked: sketch._detect_project_binary_names scrapes every\n`name = \"...\"` in Cargo.toml (including dependency tables) and keeps only the\nLAST component of the go module path — caddy needs the full\ngithub.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2. Wrong question."  # 9824
