- op: discuss  # e307
  at: '2026-08-27T20:45:19Z'  # e307
  by: agent  # e307
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e307
  clock: 5  # e307
  nonce: e307  # e307
  message: "SATISFIED in PR #580 (merged, dev at bd62f9330d). Re-verified on the MERGED\ntree, on the item's own repro and its own instrument (recomputing the gate over\neach repo's cached survey):\n\n  bellman  15 -> 14   no longer reports  bellman.VerificationError\n  caddy   158 -> 151  no longer reports  github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2\n                      + caddyconfig/caddyfile, internal/metrics,\n                        modules/caddyhttp, modules/caddypki, modules/caddytls,\n                        notify   (4 named in the item, 3 it had not found)\n  Nothing ADDED in either arm.\n\nMECHANISM. A third first-party test, supply_chain.collect_first_party_package_names,\nreads the published name from Cargo.toml [package].name, go.mod module, and\npackage.json name; cli.cmd_verify_claims computes it once from repo_root and\nthreads it to both coverage call sites the way higher_fidelity_available does.\nThe match is component-bounded, so `caddy` cannot swallow `caddyserver` nor\n`bellman` swallow `bellmanx` -- pinned by tests, because suppression here can\nonly ever HIDE a real dependency.\n\nNOT VIA supply_chain_tier, per the refutation recorded above: the tier IS\npopulated (187/609/0 over 796 package nodes), but it is derived from the\nDECLARING FILE'S path, so a third-party package declared in an in-repo manifest\nreads first_party (cmake GnuTLS at tier 1). The manifest is read directly.\n\nCONTROLS, which are the actual result since this is the false-clean direction:\n  - bellman still reports blake2s_simd::Params, rand, rayon, ff.Field\n  - caddy still reports the whole Go stdlib (archive/tar, crypto/*, ...)\n  - corpus-wide over 42 surveys: 154 suppressed, 0 ADDED, 10/42 repos moved\n  - each of the 154 re-checked against its repo's DECLARED DEPENDENCIES:\n    90 collide, and all 90 are the workspace-sibling shape INV-nuzas/ADR-0041\n    D8a already rules first-party (ripgrep grep::pcre2; lila's 118 are all its\n    own ui/lib and ui/bits frontend modules).\n  - narrowing to names declared ONLY as a registry version leaves 4, in 2 repos\n    (@y-sweet/{client,react,sdk}, @simplex-chat/types): a monorepo that\n    publishes to npm and has a sub-project depend on itself BY VERSION. The\n    name is still the repo's own and the source is in the tree, so suppression\n    matches the statement; what is NOT established is whether that sub-project\n    resolves to the in-repo copy or an installed one. Disclosed in the\n    collector docstring rather than asserted away.\n\nSIZING CONFIRMED AS FILED: no verdict moves. qualifying_only is `not unknown`,\nand 14 / 151 entries still withhold both repos. This is noise reduction that\nmakes the remainder readable; the remainder is stdlib enumeration (WI-lutuh).\n\nSCOPE NOT TAKEN, filed rather than chased: Python is deliberately excluded (a\npyproject DISTRIBUTION name is not the module spelling, and path-derivation\nalready covers it). Java/pom.xml, Elixir/mix.exs, Ruby/gemspec, Swift/Package.swift\nand Haskell/.cabal are untested analogues -- 22 of the 42 corpus repos have no\ncargo/go/npm manifest name at all, several reporting hundreds of modules\n(eclipse.jdt.ls 643, ResearchKit 396, plasma-desktop 376), so whether any of\nthose counts include self-references is UNMEASURED, not known-absent."  # e307
